## 3. Verified source pack (V-1…V-7) — claim classes any proposal must get right

### V-1. "The stdlib `wave` module can read our recordings" — FALSE as stated; version-scoped
**[VERIFIED]** Python 3.14 documentation (captured 2026-10-06, capture `99a954bd498d4cd99ac4ea919437f39c`, sha256 `463a7c29…78bc5ca`, locator: page text "Only uncompressed PCM encoded wave files are supported."): the module supports uncompressed PCM only. Same page: "**Changed in version 3.12:** Support for `WAVE_FORMAT_EXTENSIBLE` headers was added, provided that the extended format is `KSDATAFORMAT_SUBTYPE_PCM`."
- Applicability: any Python-stdlib-only I/O path (a plausible bounded alternative) rejects or mishandles 32-bit float WAV, ADPCM, mp3-in-WAV, etc. Extensible-header PCM files need **Python ≥ 3.12**; on 3.11 and earlier they fail. A proposal choosing the stdlib path must state this boundary explicitly and version-pin the interpreter, and must still store an ingest verdict per file rather than silently passing metadata through.
- A proposal claiming "reads WAV" without a subtype table fails the brief's "practical supported input/output boundary" requirement.

### V-2. libsndfile as I/O backbone — supported, but claims must be version-pinned
**[VERIFIED]** libsndfile official home page, version footer "1.2.2" (capture `91d61ea8101b4b31b26cc913ff4c40a5`, sha256 `edb94f16…efdd5d527`): reads/writes "a large number" of formats, on-the-fly conversion; **latest release per repo changelog is 1.2.2 (2023-08-13)** (capture `55c4b29c…1fe91f`, CHANGELOG.md, "## [1.2.2] - 2023-08-13").
- Applicability: libsndfile is a sound I/O **boundary** choice, not a "handles every codec" guarantee; e.g. MP3 support only exists since 1.1.0 (CHANGELOG "## [1.1.0] … MPEG Encode/Decode Support", decode seeking listed as available, encode limited to Layer III). A proposal must enumerate its accepted format/subtype list and pin a minimum libsndfile version.

### V-3. Consequential real failure, followed through fix, with exact release applicability
**[VERIFIED]** libsndfile `NEWS.OLD` (capture `3a78bdab…ab05263`):
- "Version 1.0.26 (2015-11-22) … Minor bug fixes and improvements." — release in which the regression was introduced.
- "Version 1.0.27 (2016-06-19): **Fix an SF_INFO seekable flag regression introduced in 1.0.26.** … Add handling of CUE chunks. …"
- Related later seek fixes: 1.0.29 (2020-08-15) "Fix ASAN crash in `wavlike_ima_seek()`"; 1.0.30 (2020-09-18) "`sf_open_fd()` sometimes leaves filehandle open even if `close_desc` is TRUE".
- **Causal limits:** the defect window is exactly 1.0.26 → fixed in 1.0.27; behavior claims about "libsndfile reports seekable" are **false on 1.0.26, true on ≥1.0.27**. Lesson for the plan (affects waveform/cache identity and seeking design): seek behavior of the I/O layer is version-dependent; the proposal must pin **min libsndfile ≥ 1.0.27** and re-run seek conformance checks at every dependency bump, because a wrong `seekable` flag silently changes which read path (seek vs stream) a cache builder takes.
- **[Limitation preserved]** The exact fix commit hash and the specific regression-test file for the 1.0.27 fix were **not captured** in this stage (not located within the stage budget). The official `NEWS.OLD` entry plus the project's own test-suite policy ("When new bugs are found, new tests are added to the test suite to ensure that these bugs don't creep back", home page, V-2) support the issue→fix→test chain at release granularity only. Do not cite a commit hash for this fix without capturing it — that would be an invented source chain.

### V-4. Cue support has a version floor; cue *semantics* are not given by the library
**[VERIFIED]** Same `NEWS.OLD`: "Add handling of CUE chunks" appears first in 1.0.27. **[INFERENCE]** Reading/writing a CUE chunk is format plumbing; nothing captured defines what a cue means at an edit boundary, under rate conversion, or after a trim — that mapping is the proposal's own contract (brief ¶7). A proposal that treats "libsndfile handles cues" as a semantic guarantee overreaches. Also relevant: a cue table is primarily an *editorial* artifact; the sidecar-table export (not the WAV CUE chunk) should be the authoritative cue representation, with CUE-chunk round-trip as an optional fidelity extra.

### V-5. Samplerate limits are version-dependent
**[VERIFIED]** CHANGELOG (capture `55c4b29c…1fe91f`): 1.2.0 (2022-12-25) "Removed: Maximum samplerate limit … In version 1.1.0, an artificial limit of 655350 Hz was created" (issue #850).
- Applicability: any proposal sentence of the form "libsndfile caps samplerates at 655350 Hz" is **true for 1.1.0 only, false for ≥1.2.0**. Conversely "no samplerate limit" must still not be read as "all rates are validated/sensible". Version-scope every format-capability claim.

### V-6. Resource lifecycle: `sf_open_fd` regressions (twice)
**[VERIFIED]** `NEWS.OLD` 1.0.30: "`sf_open_fd()` function sometimes leaves filehandle open, even if `close_desc` parameter is TRUE". CHANGELOG 1.2.1 (2023-08-12): "`sf_open_fd`() regression, thanks @brentr (PR #950)" (fixed) — i.e. a regression shipped in 1.2.0 and fixed in 1.2.1.
- Applicability: an editor that opens long sources repeatedly for scrub/cache building must close descriptors deterministically; descriptor leaks on a 4-editor laptop translate into "too many open files" failures mid-session. The proposal should (a) pin ≥1.2.2 (current release), and (b) include a long-session descriptor-count check in validation. **[INFERENCE]** from the two upstream regressions; the check itself is proposed/UNEXECUTED.

### V-7. Audacity precedent — branch pinning is mandatory, and this is a research-failure mode to check
