## 4. Executed bounded check (W-1) — rounding, cumulative drift, and clipping

**[EXECUTED — candidate]** Receipt `exec-z8lwmo7y`, exit 0, 2026-10-06T08:18:04Z, code sha256 `77ebc3f7…daa31`, stdlib-only arithmetic, no audio library, no files, no network. Limits: 5 s wall / 256 MiB / no network namespace. Scope limit: an isolated arithmetic check; **it is not evidence about any editor application.**

Honest results, including the null results:

| Case | Input | Result | Reading |
|---|---|---|---|
| A | 1 s boundary, 44100→48000, floor vs round vs nearest | all → 48000 (exact) | **No divergence at this boundary** — the mapping is exact here. A proposal may not claim this specific point as a rounding hazard; boundary policy must be demonstrated on non-integer mappings. |
| B | cue at 1 h 02 m 03.5 s, 48000→44100→48000 round-trip (round-half-up both ways) | drift 0 frames | **No drift at this point** — single-point round trips can be lossless. |
| C | 100 clips × 96001 frames each, per-clip 48000→44100 rounding vs one-shot conversion | per-clip total 8 820 100 vs one-shot 8 820 092 → **+8 frames (~0.18 ms)** | The load-bearing result: **per-clip rounding accumulates**. Concatenating independently rounded clip lengths yields a mix whose total length differs from the single-authoritative mapping. This is precisely the brief's warning that "a numerical time calculation … is not evidence that the final audio content was assembled correctly." |
| D | float 1.4 → int16 naive wrap vs clamp | wrap → **−19663**; clamp → 32767 | A hot sample above full scale wraps to a large negative value if conversion casts before clamping; preview gain applied pre-limit can push peaks over 1.0. The mixdown contract must define clamp-before-quantize and where the preview gain sits relative to it. |

Design consequences (all **[INFERENCE]** from W-1 + brief ¶7): (1) the project must have **one authoritative mapping function** (integer sample arithmetic in the *project rate* frame, with a single documented rounding rule at clip edges — e.g. half-open intervals with floor for start/end so abutting clips cannot overlap or gap), never per-clip ad-hoc conversions; (2) cue positions must be stored as integers in the space they refer to (source frames or project frames) with the conversion applied once, at the edge where the space changes; (3) exported cue positions and the mix must come from the same pass so C-class drift cannot split them.

